    "When S points to the honge tree outside the window, the hair on her arms stands on end. Her eyes sparkle and she speaks with a breathless excitement. I peer out of the window and try to match the beats of her enthusiasm. But what I see is just a tree, a towering brown tree shedding its leaves, on its way towards a winter baldness.",
    "S and her husband spent a significant chunk of their life in a large gated community on the outskirts of Bengaluru. The neighbourhood boasted of gabled roofs, Spanish style villas and ornamental gardens. The common areas were floodlit and the landscaping had a predictable quality. Even if they experienced a slight incompatibility with the complex and its ethos, it felt safe and convenient.",
    "Decades later, when the children were older, they found their way to a home that was closer to their heart. A home that used exposed brick, natural stone, tiled roofs and other aesthetic elements that aligned with their own taste and sensibility. But falling in love with the rugged charm of the home and the setting was one thing, moving in and actually living here was another.",
    "‘When we moved in late 2021, on the cusp of the second wave of COVID, we felt eerily uncomfortable.’ The fear of snakes, of dimly-lit spaces, of the wild sprawl of flora and fauna meant that they did not step out of their homes after dark for the first six months. The general atmosphere of distrust fuelled by the virus too had something to with it. They moved around the community a little circumspectly. Nothing much in the natural landscape left an impression; S certainly did not take note of the honge tree.",
    "Over time, the fears receded and they found their footing in the community. They began to sip their morning tea on a stone bench in the central well area of the cluster homes. Sitting beside the honge tree and quietly witnessing its annual cycle of shedding and renewing became a ritual.",
    "The internet tells me the honge or the Indian beech tree is native to Bengaluru. It is not a showy tree like the <em>Tabebuia rosea</em> (also known as the pink trumpet tree) whose overarching blossoms have now come to represent an iconic image of the city. Honge is a hardy local tree that does not really stand out except in spring, when tiny pale-pink flowers dot the canopy, filling the air with a heady smell.",
    "In winter, the leaves start falling until the tree is completely threadbare. The dry leaves have to be swept up again and again much to the chagrin of those in charge of keeping the premises tidy. The branches wear a weary, forlorn look, bereft of any sign of vitality. By March, once winter ebbs and the temperature rises, the honge comes back to life, sprouting fresh green leaves in a blaze of spring glory.",
