    "How does a community come into being? Can it be attained willfully through the forced camaraderie of team-building activities, or the performativeness of large-scale celebrations? Or is it something you stumble upon? The incidental and unpredictable outcome of locating diverse people in the same physical or virtual geographies.",
    "For the longest time, community was not a big concern for B. Her first home in Bengaluru was an apartment block on the genial streets of Jayanagar 9th block. She knew hardly anyone in the building beyond the family that lived in the opposite flat. She and her husband were in the busy middle of life, raising a family, chasing their careers, flying in and out of cities. There was no time or inclination for neighbourly interactions.",
    "A few years down the road, they made the radical decision to abandon the city and move to Hosur where they could cultivate the land and live a life in greater synergy with nature. In the next ten years, they succeeded in building a farm but faltered in forging connections with people in the vicinity. Perhaps, it was the standalone house, the cultural differences or the unfamiliar milieu. Ultimately, a foiled robbery attempt convinced them that this lifestyle was unsafe and impractical. They moved back to Bengaluru, settling into this green neighbourhood complex on the city’s periphery that offered both security and open expanses.",
    "This time around, B decided that community was a priority. Older and unable to travel frequently to the city to meet friends or attend events, it became important to find connections with people right where they lived – connections that tread the fine line between friendliness and respect for each other’s privacy.",
