    "When I ask N why she started walking, her answer is similar. She was already exercising so she did not regard it as fitness. As the founder of a design and craft-centric company, she worked from home and spent all day talking to people on the phone. The thirty-minute outing at the end of the day was meant to break the monotony of indoors, to come face-to-face with the natural world.",
    "N had always longed for open spaces. She grew up in the dense mayhem of Purasawalkam in central Chennai where tightly-packed houses stood next to or above shops and markets. While cycling to school every day, she took the longer route simply to pass through a neighbourhood filled with trees. She had her favourite: a tree with a marbled trunk and a sprawling canopy.",
    "Once N started working, life moved to the big cities – Ahmedabad, Pune, Mumbai – and homes became high-rise apartments. She ran or hiked through the streets lined with concrete buildings and crawling with vehicular traffic breathing smoke and dust. But this was not the life N and her husband wanted for themselves or for their young daughter. They wanted green landscapes, exposure to arts and crafts and the option of alternative schooling. Eventually, when they renounced the big city life and arrived in this lush neighbourhood in Bengaluru’s suburbs, their daughter gasped. She thought they were setting up camp inside a forest.",
