    "I love that this mother-daughter walk is more about stopping than moving forward. It is about bestowing attention and inquiry on the road rather than simply traversing it. The sentiment is familiar. On my own route, there are fixed phenomena that I audit regularly like the emergence of <em>konna’s</em> chandelier-like flowers or the advent of winter’s first crisp chill. The walk is hardly about the number of steps covered or the imaginary kilos, if any, lost. As for N, it is a pretext to exit the confines of my mind and renew contact with the world. To feel the compact but brief joy of a quick glance, a brief exchange, a quietly slipping sun."
