    "It is only four pm in the afternoon but the room is already turning dark. S switches on a tall, square floor lamp so that I can make my notes. She tells me that she has gotten used to this, the snug, dimly-lit interiors of her home.",
    "‘I have always wanted a house with a staircase. Like the lines from <em>Fiddler on the Roof</em>: “There would be one long staircase just going up, And one even longer coming down…”’ I don’t know the song she is referring to but the aspiration for a staircase is mutual. Having lived in single-storied homes and apartments, I have always pined for the luxury and mystery of a flight of stairs.",
    "A few years ago, S was visiting friends who lived in a residential complex in the city’s suburbs. Her friend was standing on the terrace when he spotted them turning into the drive way. Before coming down to greet them he plucked two chickoos from the tree overhanging his terrace, came down and offered it to them.",
    "S was smitten. A house with a staircase and fruit hanging from the terrace. What more could anyone want?",
    "Their decision was made. S and her husband, approaching retirement age, decided to move from the familiar clamour of Kolkata where they had spent almost all their adult life to the quiet green suburbs of Bengaluru. From the verticality of apartment life, it was a welcome change to live closer to the ground, to be able to step outside and feel the earth under their feet.",
    "S is inherently personable and soon made friends, became part of interest groups (knitting, bird watching) and melded into community life. In continuation of the work that she had already been doing in Kolkata, she started an activity centre to work with women in the locality. She taught them to speak English, to stitch and crochet; she created a space where they could find comfort in each other’s company.",
    "In the midst of all this activity, I ask her if there is a ritual or a small moment in her every day that she looks forward to.",
    "‘Yes, as soon as I wake up, I step out and admire the sita ashoka tree’s orange and yellow blooms. I love to watch the little birds that come to the top and create a ruckus. Or more recently I have taken to lying in bed and looking out of the window. I don’t get up until I can see a bird flying across the sky. I tell myself, let me watch one bird before I cede to the demands of the day.’",
    "Watching the birds is not a new interest for S. It is an extension of the life they have always lived, visiting the botanical gardens in Kolkata to spot and adore these creatures. Yet, now the birdwatching means more.",
    "‘It has been tough, the last couple of years. You look for things to anchor you and make it worthwhile to get up in the morning.’",
    "I feel the weight of those words without her having to say anything more.",
    "Not just the birds, the community work S is involved in is also part of this desire for meaning. ‘I believe each day has to be worth something. Not anything grandiose. It could be something small, just to make my today worthwhile for me.’",
    "S’s words resonate deeply with me.",
    "I go home and ponder on how when you are young ‘meaning’ is found in the big things like professional achievement or social change or transformative travel. But as you grow older, you realise that meaning-making happens in the day-to-day, via deeds or routines that are small-scale, personal and accretive. Like watching a bird soar the sky or a tree burst into bloom or teaching someone to crochet. You pile up these tiny joys, collect beautiful odds and ends that work their alchemy on you, maybe only you, and that too briefly. And that’s how life goes on."
