    "V is wearing a plain black t-shirt with the Spanish spelling for football (‘Futbol’) printed on it. But I notice it only half-way through our conversation when his eyes light up talking about all the games he played with the other children in their own version of the Chinnaswamy Stadium: a perfectly round lawn ringed by houses, resembling a central pitch with surrounding galleries.",
    "In the broad open area of this community, they broke window panes and tried ludicrous stunts like kicking the ball over the roofs into the Vrishabawathi river. V was among a group of ten or twelve kids who grew up together and treated the premises with both the affection and disregard so typical of childhood. By the time he was eight, football had already cemented its place in V’s life, pushing every other sport down the ranks. Whenever there was a toss-up between cricket and football, he campaigned for football. Cricket, with its complicated rules and the ever-present risk of losing the ball and having to search for it, held little appeal. Football, in contrast, was simple, direct, and free of any gear requirements.",
    "During the lockdown, while city folk stayed imprisoned in apartments viewing the city with wistful eyes, V and his friends roamed free and wild inside the compound wall. If until then he complained to his parents about the distance from the city, from friends, about the half an hour journey (to the metro station or bus stop) to start the journey to anywhere, now his grouses became relief and gratitude.",
    "‘At one point during COVID, there were kids from the ages of six to twenty-two all playing together. My mother was really worried that some of the little ones would get hurt. But everyone wanted to play and that is all that mattered.’",
