   slug: "an-aged-love",
   number: 11,
   title: "An Aged Love",
   image: "an-aged-love.jpg",
   imageW: 1287,
   imageH: 1802,
   imageAlt: "Watercolor of a terrace lined with bonsai trees on stone benches",
   imageAfterParagraph: 4,
   paragraphs: [
    "I am sitting in Dr J’s living room where a large window overlooks a shaded courtyard. In the middle of the courtyard, the ripples of a green pond can be seen and all around it the miniature beauty of bonsai trees. Dr J tells me his love for gardening goes back to his childhood in Bhilai. In the steel township, officer’s bungalows sat amid vast gardens, plots separated not by boundary walls but by hedges. These green spaces have travelled with the surgeon wherever he has gone.",
    "Dr J began working on bonsai decades ago from an ‘innovation born out of necessity’ kind of scenario. Their apartment had neither a balcony nor a terrace. Only a window ledge about eight inches wide making bonsai pots the only feasible option for a garden. In the 1980s much before the dawn of internet, Dr J juggled volumes on surgery along with illustrated books on bonsai, piecing together the foreign knowledge required to cultivate these diminutive trees. Over time, it became an abiding love.",
    "Once their children moved away and they had settled into a slower rhythm, Dr J and his wife R began to look for a home further away from the city with enough space for their bonsai containers. This community right on the city’s rim, checked all the boxes. One look at the abundant green cover and Dr J was sold. As he declares with a grin ‘It was LAFS - love at first sight.’",
    "The garden wraps itself around their home serving as the true scene of action; the home only its corollary. Dr J walks me through his densely-packed backyard and the spotless terrace filled with varieties of ficus, cycads, and pines arranged neatly on low stone benches. Since the ‘trees’ are contained in a limited amount of soil, they need to be watered daily and to be observed continually for any kind of stress or infestation. It takes a minimum of three to four years for a plant to take shape, and he has plants that are over thirty years old. He points to a Jade bonsai, to the weathered quality of its trunk and emphasizes that ‘It takes time for all that character to come.’ Clearly, instant and effortless gratification has no place in the bonsai landscape. It demands a long-term view, an old-world dedication and restraint.",
    "When I look around their home, I see this philosophy reflected in every corner. In the hand-embroidered cut-work runners that I recognize from my grandmother’s time, in the dull pieces of brassware that are family heirlooms, in the ancient rosewood cots reimagined into low-lying sofas. Everything has a settled, lived-in quality and reflects a respect for antiquity. Probably one more reason why they felt at home in this community that had the same urge to revive and recast objects that everyone had given up on. Like the coloured glass bottles embedded in walls, the broken tiles arranged to create designs in a gazebo, the repurposed wooden doors and windows.",
    "Towards the end of our conversation R points out that the community caters to every interest conceivable – birdwatching, crochet, snake rescue – name an interest and there is a group for it. There is a gardeners’ group but, oddly, no bonsai group. She hints that maybe that is a seed for Dr J to sow. He laughs but somehow it feels as if he would much rather spend time absorbed in his garden than managing a crowd on WhatsApp. Even among like-minded people, his connection with the bonsai remains private and self-contained.",
    "Their delicate care of the bonsai and their reverence for its time-worn quality is inspiring. In a society obsessed with erasing all evidence of aging starting with our own skin, it is a useful reminder that age is a record of life, of history, of the many turmoils one must endure to attain a certain milestone. It is earned. Like the bonsai perhaps we need to wear it more proudly, as a marker of our vintage."
   ]
