   slug: "return-to-self",
   number: 12,
   title: "Return to Self",
   image: "return-to-self.jpg",
   imageW: 1421,
   imageH: 1993,
   imageAlt: "Watercolor of a lamplit path between homes at night",
   imageAfterParagraph: 7,
   paragraphs: [
    "He speaks so softly that I’m half afraid the voice recorder will not catch our conversation. A guitarist in a renowned band that combines Carnatic music with progressive rock, P has a remarkably gentle demeanour. Far from thundering across stages, he comes across as someone who has never raised his voice in his life.",
    "P grew up in Neyveli, a town in Tamil Nadu known for its lignite mines and thermal power plants. The videos of the place on YouTube convey both the idyllic quality of a village and the symmetry of a planned township: clean, wide roads, neatly divided blocks and an abundance of trees. Since the central government owns and manages the place, private development is restricted. It reminds me of Kolar Gold Fields and the BEML township where my husband grew up. In his mind, that town exists as some kind of utopia untouched by the privations of modern life.",
    "When P speaks about Neyveli, I sense the same nostalgia. He recalls that growing up it was quiet and unhurried, a world unto itself. Even the whirr of a single motorbike on the road stood out. When P visited recently, twenty-five years later, he found that nothing had changed.",
    "He left Neyveli for college, where he taught himself to play the guitar, joined a music club and eventually became part of a band that pioneered a musical genre in the country. He and his wife settled down in Bengaluru in an apartment complex in Indira Nagar that offered access to amenities but felt congested.",
    "Post COVID, they began looking for a place more tranquil and slightly removed from urban life’s discord. In this suburban community where his in-laws already lived, they found a home that was not just visually appealing but also offered the ambience they were seeking. The duplex gave him the opportunity to set up a small recording space.",
    "‘It is a very minimal setup, just a table really. In the other house, one could always hear noise. Vessels being washed or vehicles passing. But here I have the luxury of space and the insulation from disturbances to practise and create music.’",
    "At night, when the roads are empty, the houses are dark, and there is little risk of running into people you have to make small talk with, he steps out with his wife to stroll down the street listening to music.",
    "‘The calmness and the emptiness open my mind to think differently or more creatively. An idea or a spark will emerge and over the next two or three days I work on it, letting it gradually come to surface.’",
    "It is obvious that these nocturnal jaunts are an attempt to simulate the cocooned seclusion of his childhood, cut off from the thrills and enticements of the outside world. It makes me marvel at how he reconciles his career in music performing in front of hundreds of thousands of fervent fans with this craving for slowness and quietude.",
    "In his mind, there is no contradiction. When he is performing, he cannot see the crowd. The lights are on the stage and the audience beyond the first two rows is just a mass of darkness. He plays blind, focused only on the notes made by the instrument. Even when the performance is external, he seems to be immersed in a world of his own making.",
    "In the course of putting this series together, I’ve now heard several people talk about their ‘walk’ as a centering ritual. For most, including me, the walk is a way to step outside the self, to make contact with the physical world, to be energized by its grandeur and movement. I suspect that for P, it may well be the opposite. The walk at night is a return to self, to the neutrality of silence, to the simplicity of a lost world."
   ]
